// The boss journal: Vhalgrath, the Ember Wyrm, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).wyrm = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Past the road the world is bigger than any wall I have held. The dragon follows whoever holds him, then leaves the ground altogether and comes down where he pleases. A line cannot hold what is not there. I learned to watch the sky. Gave ground.",
    gloomsworn: "Out past the road there are skies without ceilings. The Wyrm follows whoever stands before him, then leaves the earth and comes down wherever he pleases. I cannot guard what is not there. I looked up. I have lain under worse skies, and I was dead for those.",
    thresher: "Beyond the road everything is larger, the dragon included. Vhalgrath is weather with wings: drought on the ground, then hail from a clear sky wherever he pleases. I watched where the embers drew his road down. Nothing lingers on a hill that hot. Not yet.",
    bloodreaver: "Past the road, and finally a real fire in him! Claws like plough blades, a breath that cooks the floor, and then he flew off before I'd finished enjoying it. Cheeky. I want him back on the ground. I have never been so sure I'm in the right place.",
    runeguard: "Beyond the road the old texts run out, and the Wyrm writes his own. Two courts: the air gives plenty of warning; the ground gives none that I have yet read. I began a new volume and left the ground chapter blank. Not foreseen; now it will be.",
    gorgemaw: "Beyond the road: a roast of the first rank, a great black beast with fire in the belly, served from the sky. I am told it is too hot to eat. I shall be the judge of that. I came away hungry, and singed, and happier than I have been in weeks.",
    oathbroker: "The Tyrant holds the crater by right of age and enforces it by fire, in terms read out in the air before they land. One must be there to read them. I was not. Breach noted. Beyond the road the contracts are older and the penalties better funded.",
    ironpalm: "He came down from the sky and spoke with fire. I answered too slowly. The ground here is warm and uneven, and the road is far behind us. Listen to the wings before the claws. I will remember. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Big. Black. Flies when it chooses, lands where it likes. No line on a thing that won't stay on the ground. Watched where it came from, not where it went. New country, bigger quarry. I'm not complaining. Much.",
    shadowblade: "The mark has the whole sky to hide in, which is cheating. When it comes down it comes down loudly. I waited at its flank and counted its blind side. First time I have been out-shadowed by the weather. Will try again.",
    pyromancer: "A DRAGON! Fire, and he is better at it than I am! I have never been so insulted, or so delighted. The floor glowed the colour of trouble all night and I followed it like a moth. Hair intact, barely. Beyond the road is wonderful.",
    thermist: "Hot. All of him, and the ground he spat on. I glowed with admiration and burnt my boots. The sky was cold; that part I liked. Beyond the road the readings go off the page. I shall need a bigger notebook.",
    driftblade: "He took off and I wanted to follow him, which is not allowed, and then he came down like a falling mountain on the far side of the crater, and I swept through the ash for the whole of it. The road ends here and the wind does not.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man arrives by air, which is vulgar and effective. Three scorched marks in the floor, and he picks one for his entrance. The company, a long way from the road, was not cued. (My Doll was singed, and sulked.)",
    cleric: "An old tyrant on a hot hill, and nobody he did not burn a little. He is not cruel; he is simply very large. I had more to mend than I could reach, far from any road home. Dawn keep us. Back to it.",
    grovekeeper: "A drought and a fire in one animal. The ground does not take the seed where he has breathed. Everything he does comes from above, like weather, and I am not used to weather that chooses. We are far from the road, and the weeds are larger here.",
    chronomancer: "He rises, he falls, he lands, and each time he lands I am standing in the wrong place. The warning is long, the beat is exact, and I found it only on the fourth try. Larger country, same lesson. Not my first time.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one dragon, very old, very warm. Symptoms: burns, shocks, and a floor that bites for some time after. Shelves ran low by the first descent. One flask went off. Not mine! Beyond the road, other people's flasks go off. Noted.",
    threadweaver: "He hovers over the whole crater like a loose thread and drops where he pleases. My stitches kept running into the last place he had been. A sloppy seam, entirely mine, and a long way from home with no spare thread.",
    tidecaller: "Past the road the sky is the larger water, and I had not learned its tides. The Wyrm walked after the knight, then rose and came down wherever he pleased, and I mended half a swell behind him all night. Tomorrow I plan the water for his flights.",
    lanternkeeper: "Past the road the sky is the larger dark, and I had never lit it. The Wyrm came down wherever he pleased, and my lanterns were never where we needed them. Tomorrow I light where he will land, not where he has been. Keep your light.",
    kenseier: "A dragon has no rhythm worth the name: he walks, he rises, he comes down where he pleases. I spent the night learning the one beat he kept, his claws on me, and watching the sky for the rest. Tomorrow I count the Wyrm. Catch the beat.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The Wyrm is down. The line held through the flights and the burning rim, and the crater is quiet. A long way past the Barrow, and still standing. I counted heads on a warm rock and said nothing for a while. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "He is down. The crater is quiet, and I am still here, which still surprises me. A long post, and a long way from the road. Somebody else may keep the watch a while. The watch is relieved.",
    thresher: "Vhalgrath has come in at last, and the crater may go quiet. I said a few courteous words over him; he was old weather, and old weather has earned them. Something new stirs in me, not yet due. In time.",
    bloodreaver: "The big lizard is down, and every scar he gave me is still warm. The best fire I ever took, and I took all of it. I'll remember his heart opening for the rest of my life. Thank you, my friend. What's next?",
    runeguard: "The Wyrm is judged, and the air with him. I read the sky at last: every line drawn before it was walked, every fire shown before it fell. The blank chapter is filled. As foreseen, finally.",
    gorgemaw: "The roast is carved. Smoke, ash and a touch of old bone. I ate until I could not feel my teeth, and it was worth every burn. Slightly overdone. Magnificent. Beyond the road, they know how to cook.",
    oathbroker: "The Tyrant's claim on the crater is voided, and the account is closed. We paid in full, and the final instalment came due in the heart of him. Countersigned. A larger ledger now, and a lighter pen.",
    ironpalm: "He spoke his last word with fire, and we answered it from the ground. The crater is quiet now. The road is long behind us and longer ahead. Breathe.",
    ranger: "The old dragon is down. The wind in the crater is mine now. Clean line, at last. A whole sky to myself and nothing in it.",
    shadowblade: "The mark came down and stayed down. For once the sky was empty, and so was he. It never saw me, and in the end it never saw anything. Delicious.",
    pyromancer: "I outshone a DRAGON! Briefly, and only in the last moment, but it counts. My robes are a disgrace. My heart is not. Mostly unsinged, if you do not look at the sleeves.",
    thermist: "He fell, and the whole crater went still. Hot to the end, then cold, and a quiet in the middle where I like to stand. Back to the middle. At last. Readings: all of them. I shall need a week to file.",
    driftblade: "He fell out of the sky for the last time, and I flew out through the smoke and up the ribs of older kills and never once landed, and the wind went with me, all the way to the horizon.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for the star of the air. The company stood to the very end, and the Doll took the last bow. (Applause. The crater echoed it. A fine house, so far from home.)",
    cleric: "The old tyrant is at rest, and the crater is cooling. We walked down from the peaks, all of us. Thank the light, and thank every one of us who stood in it. The road home is long, but it is ours.",
    grovekeeper: "The fire is out of him, dear. The crater will take seed again, in time. Leave the ashes alone for a season. My lot came through all of it. Plant first, and let the ground do its own thinking.",
    chronomancer: "He fell, and I did not have to undo a thing. That is the first time with a dragon. I shall be proud of us, I suppose. I am now. And a little afraid of how large the next thing is.",
    alembist: "Result: one dragon, extinguished. Shelves nearly bare, every patient alive. A new reagent has appeared in the heart of the crater, not mine. Labelled: do not touch. Touched anyway. Mildly warm.",
    threadweaver: "He is unpicked at last, and the whole hem came home. There is a seam in the sky where he used to hang, and I did not tie it. Pulled it tight regardless. Needle up, and breathing again.",
    tidecaller: "Vhalgrath is down, and the crater lies quiet as a dry harbour. The water was high every time he fell on us, and it held. A long way past the road, and the sea gives back.",
    lanternkeeper: "Vhalgrath is down, and the crater is cooling. Four of us climbed out of it, and the lanterns I set were still burning when we left. A long way from any street. I'll leave one burning on the rim.",
    kenseier: "The Wyrm is down and the crater is quiet. He was the first enemy whose rests I had to learn as well as his strokes. I sat on a warm stone and let the ash fall. Ash settles.",
  },
  moves: {
    "Tyrant's Roar": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He roared, and it reached every one of us. Nowhere to stand that was not in it. I kept the line close to our healer, boots set, and took it standing. A shield means nothing against a sound. I held it up out of respect for the shield.",
      gloomsworn: "His roar went through every one of us, and no shield was any use against sound. I stood close to the one who mends and let the dark take its share of me. It was a long note. I have heard lower, from the grave.",
      thresher: "His roar falls on all of us at once, and there is nowhere to hide. I had myself topped up before his throat swelled and kept my hands busy through it. Exquisite lungs, though. Few voices carry like that.",
      bloodreaver: "The roar went through my ribs and every one of my friends' too! Nothing in it for me, shameful, but they felt it, so I stayed by the healer and let them be topped up after. Ha. He's got lungs.",
      runeguard: "His throat swells and the air shakes. The roar reaches the whole crater and no ward turns it, so each of us must be whole before it sounds. I annotated it in a steady hand. The page shook anyway.",
      gorgemaw: "The roar is a course served to the whole table at once, and I cannot eat it for anyone. So we sat close, came to it well fed, and swallowed it together. Smoky, with a long finish, like an old cheese.",
      oathbroker: "The Roar is a levy on every one of the undersigned, payable at once; no clause excuses anyone. One must arrive at it already mended. We did. He offered no terms. I entered an objection regardless.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke to all of us at once, loudly. There is no answering it. We came to it whole and let it pass through us. After, the crater hummed, and I hummed with it a little, to be polite.",
      ranger: "Roar. Reaches everyone, no cover from it. Mended before, kept drawing through it. Ears rang an hour. Worth it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark roared and it found everyone, even me, which was rude. Nothing to hide behind, so I made sure I was whole when it sounded and went back to work. Loud people are such a bore.",
      pyromancer: "The roar made my hair stand on end, and he does it better than I do! No escaping it, so I made sure I wasn't already half scorched when it came and kept casting through it. A fine voice. Needs coaching.",
      thermist: "The roar goes through everyone. Fact. Be whole before it. Then it hit and I hummed with it, rattled, still swinging, every tooth humming. Frequency: low. Volume: yes. Result: unrepeatable.",
      driftblade: "He roared across the whole crater and there was no slipping through it or round it, nowhere to go, which offends me, so I came to it whole and kept moving through the sound like a swimmer through surf.",
      marionettist: "The star opened his mouth and the whole house shook, stalls and wings alike. No exits; the cast came to it whole and played on. (The Doll did not flinch. I admired her. I flinched.)",
      cleric: "The roar fell on every one of us. I mended everyone before it came, not after, and laid a little grace over my friends as he lifted his head. Even a dragon, I think, is lonely up there.",
      grovekeeper: "The roar comes like hail: no cover, my lot all at once. The mending was growing in everyone before it broke. A dragon can shake the whole valley and not shake a well-planted root.",
      chronomancer: "The roar lands just after his throat swells. I had everyone mended in the moment before; next time, which was last time, I will start earlier. I rather enjoyed the echo. It went on a long while.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the roar, felt by everyone, no hiding from it. Remedy: the whole party whole before it sounds, the big brew poured just ahead. Front shelf, and tape down the corks; things rattle in a crater.",
      threadweaver: "The roar ran along every thread at once. No tying it off. We stood close, I pulled the slack tight, and we took it in common. Mind the slack; it is the first thing a dragon finds.",
      tidecaller: "The roar rolled over every one of us, and there is no hiding from sound. We stood close, and I let the water climb as he lifted his head, so it was already high when the roar broke. A gull rides out the loudest gale.",
      lanternkeeper: "The roar rolled over every one of us, and there is no hiding from sound. We stood close inside my glows, and I let every lantern burst as he lifted his head, so the roar fell on a party already mended.",
      kenseier: "He roared, and it reached every one of us. A blade cannot turn a sound, so we gathered by the healer and took it standing. Mended first, then still. That answers all such blows.",
    } },
    'Rending Claws': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The claws came down on me alone, a wide rake in front of him. I waited for the shoulder to drop, shield up in the last instant, and kept the others out of the arc. Claws like that make the Barrow King seem a gentleman.",
      gloomsworn: "The claws were meant for me. I watched his near shoulder and let the rake land where I stood. A wound is only a wound; mine are written down already. The rest kept wide of the arc.",
      thresher: "The claws come down on our knight, a wide rake across the front of him, a curtain drawn the wrong way. I kept to his flank, well out of the arc, and went on working his side. Not my appointment, that one.",
      bloodreaver: "The claws! A deep one across the front of him, and the wound stays good and open. I braced, took it, and answered it while it was fresh. Again, my friend, please.",
      runeguard: "He rears and the near claw lifts. A ward a breath before; the others well outside the arc in front of him. I had written the lift of the claw in the margin before it came. I count that as the first achievement of the new volume.",
      gorgemaw: "The claws are served to me alone, a heavy rake with a sauce that runs. I opened wide just before it, kept my friends off my plate, and chewed. Rare, and good. Next time, a little less char.",
      oathbroker: "The Claws are payable by whoever stands before him, and the arc runs wide. I accepted delivery behind every protection I had, the undersigned kept clear. A dragon's signature is a claw. I did not dispute it.",
      ironpalm: "He raked once, wide. I did not raise my arms until its last instant, and took half. Then I answered. Not every blow deserves a long reply.",
      ranger: "Claws. Wide arc in front of it. Kept to its flank, outside the rake, and drew again before the dust settled. Fast animal. Slow lesson.",
      shadowblade: "The mark raked at whoever faced it, which was not me. I kept to its side, where neither claw could find me, and came back in when it was done. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He raked the ground in front of him and I decided I did not need to be there! I stood off to the side and finished my spell from the edge. A good claw. Very theatrical. Not for me.",
      thermist: "Claws: wide, in front. Step to the side. Then the heat came and I swung back in at once, cutting, behind the swing and not in it. Result: unburned. Conclusion: stand wherever the claw isn't.",
      driftblade: "The rake came round in front of him and I went through his side and out the back of it and round again, never landing, and it was over before I had even slowed, like a gust through a grove.",
      marionettist: "The leading man swept the stage in front of him. The cast stepped to the wings on cue and my Doll slipped out behind. (Applause for the Doll, mostly.)",
      cleric: "The claws fell on our knight, as they always do. I laid the light on him before they landed instead of after, and he barely staggered. Old habit, young bones. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "The big rake came on like a squall, front of him only. I had planted on our knight before it, so the mending was already growing when it struck. Let the storm be late. I will be early.",
      chronomancer: "The claws come down on the beat after the shoulder drops. I warded our knight in the gap before it, and was early. Not for the first time, nor the last, nor, possibly, the current.",
      alembist: "The claws, one heavy rake, front of him only: a blue ward on whoever stands there, and everyone else kept out of the arc. Label it plainly. A heavy rake needs a heavy bottle.",
      threadweaver: "The rake landed on our knight, and it ran down my thread to the archer, so neither took all of it. A neat seam. Keep to the sides, you lot, and leave the front to him.",
      tidecaller: "The claws were meant for the knight alone, a wide rake in front of him, so the rest of us kept to his sides. I watched his shoulder and had the water high around the knight before it fell, not after.",
      lanternkeeper: "The claws were for our knight alone, a wide rake in front of him, so the rest of us kept to his sides. I had a lantern hung on the knight before the blow and kept its wick gold.",
      kenseier: "The claws came down on me alone, a wide rake before him. I met them with the blade as they landed, and the others stayed out of the arc. Each rake had the same count, and by the end I answered it without thinking.",
    } },
    'Ember Breath': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He breathed at one of us, not me, and the fan of fire followed them. I kept the line clear of the long flames and off the glowing ground it left behind. Fire does not respect a post. I do.",
      gloomsworn: "The breath chose someone else, and followed them. I kept clear of its length and off the scorched floor after, which lingers. I know something of lingering.",
      thresher: "The breath chooses one of us and follows them to the last. Whoever it is waits, then steps aside late, not back. The ground it scorches stays foul a good while; I kept off it as I would a pyre still smoking.",
      bloodreaver: "A cone of fire! And it follows whoever he picks until the last moment. I like that. Step aside late and sideways, and keep off the molten ground after. A fine scar, not mine.",
      runeguard: "His head goes back and the fire gathers; it tracks its quarry until the last. Leave the fan sideways, late, and stay off the molten ground it leaves. I noted that the breath obeys the rule of a good argument: it follows until the last possible moment.",
      gorgemaw: "The breath is a flambé, served at one guest and following them round the table. Step out of the line at the last, and do not stand on the plate afterwards. Overcooked, but fashionably so.",
      oathbroker: "The Breath is served on one named party, follows him, and then lingers on the floor. Clear the line, then clear the ground. Two clauses, one injury.",
      ironpalm: "Fire came for one of us. It followed. I did not run; I stepped aside, late. Then I stayed off the hot ground.",
      ranger: "Breath at one of us. Tracks till late. Stepped out sideways at the last, not back. The floor burns a while after. Don't stand on it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark breathed at someone else, which was lovely of it. The fire follows them until the last moment, so they step aside late. The ground burns afterwards. I stayed well clear of the floor.",
      pyromancer: "He breathed fire at one of us and I felt I should have been consulted! It follows them until the last instant; step off sideways and keep clear of the glowing floor. Professional jealousy aside, a very fine cone.",
      thermist: "Breath: hot, narrow, tracking. Step aside, late. Then the ground stays molten, stay off it! I wasn't cold enough once and stepped on it. Result: boots reduced to a theory. Bootless, but resolved.",
      driftblade: "The fire came after one of us in a long sweep and I cut through its edge and out the side just as it set, and the floor behind it was glowing and I went round that too.",
      marionettist: "The star breathed at one member of the cast, and the beam followed them across the stage. Sidestep at the last cue; the boards afterward burn for a time. (The Doll walked round them.)",
      cleric: "The fire hunted one of my flock and followed her across the floor. Wait, I told her, and step aside late, and mind the floor after. Child, you are faster than you think. She was.",
      grovekeeper: "The breath comes like a grass fire, dear, in a line after one of my lot. Wait, step aside, and keep off the scorched ground; nothing grows there for a time. Plant after, never on.",
      chronomancer: "The fire tracks its quarry until the last breath. I stepped aside on that note and not before. The ground burned for a time after. Next time, which was last time, I stood further off.",
      alembist: "A cone of fire at one of us, tracking, and a floor that burns afterwards: step aside late, then keep off the glow. Label the floor too: this side hot.",
      threadweaver: "The fire chased one stitch across the floor. Wait for the last moment, step out sideways, and keep every thread off the glowing ground. A burnt hem is a sloppy hem.",
      tidecaller: "The fire hunted one of us and followed whoever it chose across the floor. They waited for the last moment and stepped out sideways; the rest kept clear of its length, and all of us kept off the glowing ground it left. Scorched floor stays hot a long while.",
      lanternkeeper: "The fire hunted one of us and followed them across the floor. They waited until the last moment and stepped out sideways. The rest kept clear of its length, and I set my lanterns well away from the scorch it left.",
      kenseier: "He breathed at one of the others and the fan of fire followed them. We kept clear of the long flames and off the glowing ground it left. It is a long stroke, not a quick one. Plenty of time to leave.",
    } },
    'Tail Lash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He swept the tail round behind him, not in front. I held my place at his front and the line stood to his sides. Whoever stood at his back learned it quickly. A tail is a rear guard of its own.",
      gloomsworn: "The tail swung behind him. I stood before his face, the rest at his flanks. Whoever stood at his back learned it the way I learned dying: once.",
      thresher: "The tail sweeps round behind him in a long, slow arc. I work from his side, never his back, and it passes me by. Whoever stood behind him learned the lesson once.",
      bloodreaver: "The tail! Behind him, a wide sweep, and I was in front for once. A good one, but not mine. The ones behind him were not so pleased. Stay at his sides.",
      runeguard: "His shoulders turn and the tail rises. The sweep is behind him, so the line stands at his sides or his front, and no one behind. Rear guard is a poor posting. I have never advised anyone to take it.",
      gorgemaw: "The tail is a side dish, served at the back of the table. Whoever sat at the back got the lot. I sat at the head, as a gourmand should. Underseasoned.",
      oathbroker: "The location of the Tail is the only term that matters: behind him, never in front. Those who stood behind were in breach. The undersigned stood to the sides. A dragon's clauses are not hidden; they swing.",
      ironpalm: "He swung the tail behind him. I stood in front and did not move. Those behind moved.",
      ranger: "Tail. Behind it, wide arc. Kept to its flank and shot over the shoulder. Anyone who stood behind it knows by now.",
      shadowblade: "The mark swung its tail behind it, which is where I usually stand. Rude. Moved to its side, where neither end can see me. A new rule: never trust the other end.",
      pyromancer: "He swung that tail behind him and I was standing in just the wrong place, glowing! Learned to stand at his side. A little bruised. Dragons ought to announce their rear.",
      thermist: "Tail: behind him. Step to the side. Then the heat came and I was swinging back in, from the flank, not the rear. The rear is an unsolved problem. I gave up on it.",
      driftblade: "The tail came round behind him in a great arc and I went through the gap beside it, out the front and round again, and never behind him, because behind is where birds get caught.",
      marionettist: "The star's tail swept the upstage. I had my Doll in the wings and the cast at his sides, where the arc does not reach. (A respectable review.)",
      cleric: "The tail swept behind him and caught a friend who had wandered there. I told her to keep to his sides, and mended what I could. Next time she will listen. Next time they all will, I think.",
      grovekeeper: "The tail comes round like a storm cloud behind him. Keep to his flanks and the weather passes you by. My lot learned it the first time.",
      chronomancer: "He turns his shoulder, and the tail comes round a moment later, behind him. I was already at his side. I knew it, I think. This time I did.",
      alembist: "The tail, a wide sweep behind him: stand at his flanks or his front and nowhere in the arc. The shelf behind him is not safe either, I found. Moved it.",
      threadweaver: "The tail swept his back, and anyone stitched in there came away torn. Mind the seam. My stitches stand at his flanks now.",
      tidecaller: "The tail swept the ground behind him, where nobody with sense ought to be. We kept to his front and his flanks, and the ones who wandered round his back came away rinsed. The stern of a ship is the part that swings.",
      lanternkeeper: "The tail swept the ground behind him, where nobody with sense stands. We kept to his front and his flanks, and my lanterns went there too, never at his back.",
      kenseier: "He swept the tail behind him, not before. I kept my place at his front, and the others stood to his sides. Whoever stood at his back learned the beat quickly. A tail is a second blade, and it sits where the first is not.",
    } },
    'Magma Burst': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The marks came up over the others, not me. I held his front while each took their own patch of floor and stepped off their own pool when it struck. A quiet job, for once: keeping him pointed away.",
      gloomsworn: "Marks rose over every head but mine. Each of them took a place apart and left the pool when it burst, and no one needed me for it. A rare quiet job.",
      thresher: "A ring rose over each of us but our knight, and every one wanted its own ground. I walked out to mine, apart from the others, let it fall, then stepped off the pool it left. Poor footing, that.",
      bloodreaver: "Little circles of fire over my friends, and each leaves a puddle of molten rock. I stayed with the big lizard and let them handle theirs. Step out of your puddle, fast. I kept grinning and let them hop.",
      runeguard: "Marks over each of us, save the one in front. Each takes a patch of their own, apart, then leaves the pool behind. A beautifully orderly torment. I numbered the pools, briefly, before they cooled.",
      gorgemaw: "The marks were little canapés over my friends' heads, each landing in a puddle of sauce. I did not eat one. Each took their own plate and left it afterwards. Hot, hot, hot.",
      oathbroker: "Each marked party owes a circle of his own, and the pool remains after. Do not share, and do not stand in your own debt. Each liability is several, not joint.",
      ironpalm: "The marks came over the others. They parted. Each stood alone, and each stepped out of what they left.",
      ranger: "Marks over the others. Fanned out, one patch each. Stepped out of the pools after. I stayed on the dragon. Lucky draw.",
      shadowblade: "The marks went over everyone but me, and I spent the moment thinking how nice that was. Each of them took their own patch, then left the pool behind. Nobody looked at me. Heaven.",
      pyromancer: "Marks over my head, which I took as a compliment! Each of us needed our own patch of floor, and each leaves a pool of molten rock. Stepped out of mine quickly. Hem: sacrificed.",
      thermist: "Marks. Apart, each alone. Then the pool underfoot: out of it at once! I was in mine too long, boiled over, and had to be mended. Moral: move before the experiment finishes.",
      driftblade: "The marks came and I swept to my own patch at the far edge, and the others fanned out, and when it struck I was already moving, out of the pool and through the gap and round again.",
      marionettist: "Each of us got our own spotlight, and each spotlight leaves a hot patch on the boards. The cast fanned out wide, and left their marks at once. (My Doll was spared the lights.)",
      cleric: "The marks fell on my flock, each on their own. I told them: apart, and out of the pool you leave behind. They did, bless them, with only one scorched boot among them.",
      grovekeeper: "The marks came like scattered seed, one over each of my lot. Everyone took their own ground, and when it fell they stepped out of the molten patch.",
      chronomancer: "The marks appear, and a moment later they land, pool and all. I had stepped off mine by then. I did. I will.",
      alembist: "Marks over each of us, then a puddle of molten rock: apart from each other for the mark, then off the puddle at once. Keep the flasks well apart, too.",
      threadweaver: "Each mark wanted its own seam, so I cut the threads before they snapped and tied them again after we regrouped. And off the molten patch. Mind the slack.",
      tidecaller: "Marks rose over every head but the knight's. Each of us walked off to a patch of floor of our own, away from the rest, and stepped out of the pool the moment it broke, for the pools stay. A quiet shore is a safe one.",
      lanternkeeper: "Marks rose over every head but the knight's. Each of us went to a patch of floor alone, and stepped out of the pool the instant it burst, since the pools stay. I made sure no lantern of mine sat in one.",
      kenseier: "Marks rose over the others and left me alone. I kept his head turned away from them while they each went to their own patch and stepped clear of the pool as it landed. A quiet duty: only holding his eyes.",
    } },
    'Cinder Feast': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He marked one of us for a hit that had to be shared. I closed the line on them and took my share, standing in the ring, and we split it. Duty is lighter in numbers. I would not say so to a recruit.",
      gloomsworn: "One of us wore a ring to be shared. I walked in beside them, the rest followed, and the weight was cut into pieces small enough to carry. Nothing is heavy that many hands bear.",
      thresher: "A blue ring over one of us, a weight no one should carry alone. All of us in beneath it together, and it fell light. Leave a hand out and the rest carry more.",
      bloodreaver: "A blow too big for one! We all piled in on the marked one and split it between us. A fine scar each. Stand close, stand in the ring, and bleed together.",
      runeguard: "A ring over one of us, blue at the edge. It is a share, so everyone stands in it together, and it is divided among us. A reading of kindness, from a dragon. I shall not expect another.",
      gorgemaw: "A dish for the whole table, to be shared, the ring marked on the floor. We all sat down in it together and took a portion each. Large, and filling.",
      oathbroker: "The Feast is a debt owed in common: the undersigned stand in the ring together, and it is divided among them. Nobody absents himself. The first honest joint liability of my career.",
      ironpalm: "A blow for one, spoken to all. We stood in the ring together and listened together.",
      ranger: "Ring over one of us, blue. Share it: all of us in the ring at once. Everyone had to stand still. I managed.",
      shadowblade: "The mark chose one of us and I stood in the ring with the others, which I hate. It was shared among us, and thin by the time it reached me. Crowds have a use after all.",
      pyromancer: "A hit too big to take alone! We all stood in the ring together, which I resented, then I admitted it was right. It was shared between us. A wonderful hum afterwards.",
      thermist: "The ring on one of us. Hot, big, shared. Everyone in, together, close. I sweated with fright and stayed in. Equal parts division and courage.",
      driftblade: "The hit we had to share meant standing still with everyone, which I hate, so I circled the ring until the very last breath and then slipped in among them and took it.",
      marionettist: "The star pointed at one of the cast and demanded a shared bow. The entire troupe crowded into the ring and took it as one. A tolerable second act.",
      cleric: "He marked one of my flock for a great hit, and we all gathered round her in the ring, so it was divided. I stood in it with them. A burden shared is a smaller thing, and a kinder one.",
      grovekeeper: "A great blow came to one, and we all gathered under it like trees in a storm and split the weight, dear. Everyone in the ring. Stand close.",
      chronomancer: "The ring blooms, and a moment later it lands. I was inside it before, with the others, so it came divided. Nearly nobody was undone.",
      alembist: "One of us marked for a blow too large to bear alone: everyone in the ring at once, to divide the dose. Mind the pool, too. Shared bottles, shared burns.",
      threadweaver: "The ring wanted all of us together, so I tied the threads in close and we stood in it as one. Divided among us, it was nothing.",
      tidecaller: "He marked one of us for a blow too heavy to bear alone, and the blue ring on the floor was the answer. We all stood in it together, I too, and shared it into a size the water could mend. Many drops, one tide. Mind the tide.",
      lanternkeeper: "He marked one of us for a blow too heavy to bear alone, and the blue ring on the floor was the answer. We all stood in it together, and I put a lantern in the middle. Four of us, one ring, the blow split four ways.",
      kenseier: "He marked one of the others for a hit that wanted sharing. We closed on them, I with the rest, and split it. Each took a part, and a hard stroke became a light touch.",
    } },
    'Dragonfall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He rose, and embers drew a line across the floor from the sky to a scorched pit. The line and the circle at its end were what hurt. I held the ground, clear of both. I refuse to be impressed by a falling dragon. I am a little impressed.",
      gloomsworn: "He rose out of sight, and the embers drew his road to a pit. The road hurts, and so does the ring at its end. I stood clear of both and watched him arrive on nothing.",
      thresher: "Up he went, and the embers drew his road to one of the pits, the ring showing which. I stepped off the road and well clear of the pit, and let him dig it himself. It saves me the trouble.",
      bloodreaver: "He dives! A line of embers from the edge, and a great red circle where he lands. I wanted to meet him there. I stayed off the line and out of the circle, for once, and bled elsewhere.",
      runeguard: "Embers draw a line from the edge of the crater to one of three scorched pits, and a ring blooms on the pit he has chosen. Off the line and out of the ring. The line is the sentence; the ring, its punctuation.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast arrived by air, as a roast should: a trail of embers, a plate set at one of three pits. I stepped aside for the dish and kept off the plate. Served hot.",
      oathbroker: "The Dragonfall is a summons served in the air, the place of delivery named only as he commits: one of three pits. The flight line is part of the instrument. Clear of both. The jurisdiction was the sky.",
      ironpalm: "He came from the sky along a line of fire. I watched for the ring where he would land. Then I stepped aside, to one side of the line.",
      ranger: "Dive. Ember line from the rim to one of three pits. The ring shows which. Moved out sideways, away from both. First flying thing I've wanted to shoot.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flew, and I loved it. A line of embers across the floor and a ring where it would land. I waited out of both and slipped back in the moment it touched down. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He flew right over my head and I gasped! The embers drew a line and then a ring blossomed, the colour of trouble, at one of three pits. I stepped out of both. A grand entrance. I took notes.",
      thermist: "Dive: hot line, hot ring. Out of both, to the side. Which pit, then go. I stood in the ring once and burnt. Observed: do not.",
      driftblade: "He came down the line of embers and I went across it, through the gap, away from the pit he'd chosen, and out the far side before he landed, and round again, never landing myself.",
      marionettist: "The star made his entrance from the flies, along a line of footlights and onto one of three marks. The cast cleared the line and the mark. (A grand entrance. A better exit.)",
      cleric: "The dragon dove, and I watched the line of embers and the ring that bloomed. Off the line, out of the ring, I called, and we all were, and I was prouder than a priest ought to be.",
      grovekeeper: "He came like a storm out of a clear sky, along a line of embers. I read where the ring bloomed and moved my lot out of both. Weather you can read, is weather you can leave.",
      chronomancer: "The line is drawn well before he comes, and the ring blooms on the pit he picks. I had stepped off the line at the first note. For once.",
      alembist: "A dive: a line of embers, a ring at the end of it, and three pits to pick from. Off the line and out of the ring. Label the pits: left, middle, hot.",
      threadweaver: "He cut across the crater like a pulled thread, and the ring marked where he'd stitch it down. I got every stitch off the line and out of the ring.",
      tidecaller: "He climbed out of sight, and a trail of embers drew his road across the floor to the ring where he would land. The road hurts and so does the ring, so we stood clear of both. A storm read early can be sailed round.",
      lanternkeeper: "Up he went out of sight, and a trail of embers drew his road across the floor to the ring where he would come down. Road and ring both hurt. I walked the party out of the way of both and lit nothing there.",
      kenseier: "He rose, and embers drew a line across the floor from the sky to a scorched pit. The line and the circle at its end were what hurt. I stayed clear of both. Even a falling dragon announces where he will land, which is more than most do.",
    } },
    'Twin Pass': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two lines of embers at once. He flew the first without landing, then came round on the second and came down. Clear of the first was not enough. I moved twice with no rest between. A dragon gives a man no time to form up.",
      gloomsworn: "Two roads of ember. The first he only flew over; the second he landed on. I left one, then the other, and did not rest between. Rest comes later. I have been promised it.",
      thresher: "Two roads of embers. He only flies over the first and comes down on the second, so I stepped off one, then off the other, and did not linger between. Not the resting place I would have chosen.",
      bloodreaver: "Two lines! The first he only passes over; the second ends in a ring and a landing. I moved clear of the first, then clear of the second. A cheat, I call that, and I loved it.",
      runeguard: "Two lines drawn, the later one fainter. The first is only a pass, the second ends in a landing, and both must be left. The second line is what a careless reader would miss.",
      gorgemaw: "Two courses from the air: the first only flies over the table, the second lands on it. I stepped clear of both, in order. The second was the better.",
      oathbroker: "Two writs, served in sequence. The first is a flyover with no landing; the second carries the ring. The second is drawn while the first is still being served. I paid attention to the sequence.",
      ironpalm: "Two lines. The first passed over; the second landed. I watched the first, then the second.",
      ranger: "Two lines. First he only passes. Second he lands. Out of the first, then out of the second. Do not stop after the first.",
      shadowblade: "The mark made two passes, and I admired the planning. The second line is the one that ends in a landing. I moved clear of both, in turn.",
      pyromancer: "Twice! He did it twice! A line for the pass and another for the landing, and the second one is the one that bites. I got out of the first only to find the second. Eyebrow: singed, slightly.",
      thermist: "Two passes: first only over, second down. Step clear of the first, read the second, step again. I stopped after the first once. Too far, the wrong way.",
      driftblade: "Two lines and I cut across the first, through it, and over to the clear patch, and through the second just before it lit, and round, and it was glorious.",
      marionettist: "A double entrance: the star crosses the stage once without stopping, and then returns to land on his mark. The cast must clear both. (Quick exits, all round.)",
      cleric: "Two lines in the sky. The first he only flew over; the second he landed on. Clear of the first, then clear of the second, I called out like a bellringer, and breathed again.",
      grovekeeper: "Two gusts, one after the other, and the second one is the one that lands. Everybody stepped off the first line, then off the second. Move twice.",
      chronomancer: "Two lines, two notes: the second lands once the first has gone over. I left the first, then the second. This time I did.",
      alembist: "A double dive: the first line is only a pass; the second ends in a landing. Clear of both, in that order. Label both lines. I did, in chalk, on the floor, until it burned.",
      threadweaver: "Two long threads across the crater, the first only passing through, the second tying down at a pit. We stepped clear of one and then the other. No dawdling between.",
      tidecaller: "Two roads of ember at once: he flew the first and came down on the second. We left the first road and then the second without a pause between, like stepping over two rip channels in a row. Rest came after, with the water settled.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two roads of ember at once: he flew over the first and came down on the second. We left the first and then the second without a pause between, and I set my lantern beyond both, where we would be after.",
      kenseier: "He flew two ember lines at once: the first overhead, no landing, then round onto the second to come down. Leaving the first was not enough. I moved, and moved again before I could rest. The second step is the one a lazy swordsman forgets.",
    } },
    'The Roost': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He perched on the rim and shook his burning scales onto the floor, three at a time, and two whelps came down with them. The line kept moving off the scales and put the whelps down. Standing still would have been the first mistake.",
      gloomsworn: "He perched high and shed fiery scales in threes, two whelps dropping with them. We never stopped moving off the scales, and the whelps were dead before he came down. Idle hands are for graves.",
      thresher: "He sat up on the rim and shook his burning scales loose, always one patch beneath somebody, so I kept walking. Two small whelps came down with them, and I sent them to the far shore before he dropped. Early, but due.",
      bloodreaver: "The big lizard sat on the rim and rained burning scales on us, and two little ones came to bite. Good fun. Keep moving, kill the little ones before he drops, or they go off.",
      runeguard: "He perches high and the scales fall, three at a time, always one under one of us. Keep moving, and put the whelps down before he comes to the ground, or they burst. A short text, with a very short deadline.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast sat on the rim and shook his burning crumbs onto the floor, and sent two small portions to nibble at us. Better to eat the portions than let them cool and burst. Crunchy.",
      oathbroker: "The Roost is a long recess with penalties: scales fall on named tiles, one under each party in turn, and two small bailiffs come to collect. They burst if they outlive the recess.",
      ironpalm: "He sat on the rim, high. Fire fell in threes. Two small ones came at us. I stepped aside each time, and broke the small ones before he came down.",
      ranger: "Perched on the rim. Scales fall in threes, one under someone. Two whelps. Kept moving, shot the whelps down before he drops.",
      shadowblade: "The mark perched on the rim and wept fire, three tiles at a time, and one was always under somebody. The whelps were rude. I put them down before he came to the ground.",
      pyromancer: "He sat on the rim and flaked fire onto the floor like a bonfire shedding sparks, and I loved it, and kept moving! The two little dragons must be put down before he lands. My boots are a disgrace.",
      thermist: "Scales fall hot, in threes. Step out. Whelps bite: put them down. They burst if left. I left one once. Now with an empty sleeve.",
      driftblade: "He sat on the rim and burning scales fell in threes and I wove between them, round them, through the gaps, and cut down the little dragons on the way past.",
      marionettist: "The star took the balcony and showered the stalls with fire, three at a time, one on someone each time. Two small understudies came on. The cast must dismiss them before he descends.",
      cleric: "He sat on the rim and let fire rain on us, always one patch on one of my flock, and the whelps harried them. Keep moving, and put the little ones down; I shouted it until I was hoarse.",
      grovekeeper: "It rained fire from the rim, three patches at a time, and I had my lot keep moving through it like walking in a hailstorm, dear. The whelps are saplings of fire: pull them before he lands.",
      chronomancer: "He shakes, and the scales fall just after, three at a time, always one under somebody. I was moving at the first. The whelps burst at the end if they linger, so I put them down early.",
      alembist: "The Roost: burning scales in threes, two whelps, a recess of some length. Keep moving, put the whelps down before he lands, or they burst. Shelve with the scorch kit. I have a scorch kit now.",
      threadweaver: "The scales fell like loose pins, three at a time, always one over one of mine. Keep moving, keep the whelps down; they burst if they are still there when he comes to the ground.",
      tidecaller: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales down in threes, always one over someone, and two whelps came with them. We kept walking off the scales and put the whelps down before he dropped. I fed the water small and often while it rained.",
      lanternkeeper: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales down in threes, always one over someone, with two whelps alongside. We kept walking off the scales and put the whelps down before he dropped. My lanterns trailed behind us.",
      kenseier: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales loose, three at a time, with two whelps falling among them. We kept stepping off the scales and cut the whelps down. The one who stops to watch is the one who burns.",
    } },
    'Crater Boils': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The ring of floor by the rim began to glow, and it stayed that way. From then on we fought on the inner ground only. I kept the line in from the edge for the rest of the night. A smaller yard to drill in, but a yard.",
      gloomsworn: "The edge of the floor began to glow and never stopped. We were given less room after that, so I kept us to the middle for the rest of the night. I have fought in smaller places. I have lain in smaller places.",
      thresher: "The rim went from ember to flame and never cooled, so the arena is smaller for the rest of the night. I kept to the inner ground, away from the edge, as one declines the last step of a dark stair.",
      bloodreaver: "The rim boiled, and it stays boiled. A smaller room to hit him in. I like it close. Keep in from the edge, the glow bites for good.",
      runeguard: "The rim brightens and the stone along it weeps. It does not cool, so the room is smaller for the rest of the fight. Keep from the edge. I drew a new boundary on the map and pressed the pen hard.",
      gorgemaw: "The rim was set to boil, and it does not stop. The table shrank for the rest of the meal, and the edges are too hot to touch. I sat nearer the middle. Reduced, and rich.",
      oathbroker: "The Boil is a permanent amendment: the ring of floor by the rim is forfeit for the remainder. It does not cool. We keep from the edge. Property that burns is a poor lien.",
      ironpalm: "The rim boiled. It did not stop. The room was smaller after. I stayed inside, away from the edge.",
      ranger: "Rim boils. Stays boiled. Smaller room. Kept inside the glow, away from the edge. Shorter lanes now.",
      shadowblade: "The mark cooked the rim. Nowhere to hide on the edge now, which was my favourite place. Kept to the inner floor, where he looks, and hated every step.",
      pyromancer: "The whole rim lit up like the best bonfire I have ever seen, and it stays lit! The room is smaller, and my favourite corners are lava. I stand in the middle now. Poor corners.",
      thermist: "Rim goes hot, stays hot. Smaller floor. Keep to the middle, off the edge. I stepped on it once and swung. The middle, all night.",
      driftblade: "The rim lit up, all the way round, and stayed lit, and I could not skim the wall any more, which offends me, so I flew circles in the middle instead, through everything.",
      marionettist: "The edges of the stage caught fire and stayed so. The cast was confined to the middle for the rest of the act. (The Doll, being small, did not mind.)",
      cleric: "The rim began to burn and did not stop. A smaller floor makes a closer congregation, and I kept it away from the edge, scolding gently when anyone ran too near.",
      grovekeeper: "The edge of the crater went from ember to flame, and it does not come back. Plant nothing there. My lot keep to the middle now, where the ground still takes root.",
      chronomancer: "The rim begins to glow after he stamps, and from then on it will have always been glowing. We stayed in from the edge. The room was smaller, and so was the margin.",
      alembist: "The ring of floor by the rim burns for good: stay in from the edge. Shelve nothing near it. Moved the tea. Very sad.",
      threadweaver: "The edge unpicked itself into fire, and no thread will hold there. Keep every stitch in from the rim. A smaller hem, but a cleaner one.",
      tidecaller: "The outer ring of the floor began to boil and never stopped. We kept inside it for the rest of the night, closer together, which suited me; the water goes further in a smaller pool. The rim is a shore I no longer walk.",
      lanternkeeper: "The rim of the floor began to boil and never stopped. After that we fought in a smaller circle, and I drew the lanterns in with us. The same light goes further in a smaller room. The edge is a street I no longer walk.",
      kenseier: "The ring of floor near the rim began to glow and kept glowing. After that we fought on the inner ground and never the edge. Less floor, and a floor I am used to.",
    } },
    'Twin Breath': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two fans of fire at two of us, the second a heartbeat after the first. The line watched the first go and did not step into the second. Eyes up before feet. My sergeant's rule, borrowed.",
      gloomsworn: "Two breaths, two of us, the second a moment behind. We left the first and looked before stepping, because the second was waiting there. Dying twice in a row is not on my list.",
      thresher: "Two breaths at two of us, the second a beat behind the first. I stepped out of the first and looked before I stepped again, because the second was waiting right where I meant to go. Two verdicts in one hour.",
      bloodreaver: "Two cones, two targets, one behind the other! The trick is not to leap out of the first right into the second. I stayed with the big lizard and let them dance.",
      runeguard: "Two heads of the same breath, the second a beat behind. Leave the first, and look before you step, for the second may be waiting. A sentence with a subordinate clause, and the clause is the dangerous part.",
      gorgemaw: "Two flambés, one after the other, at two different guests. Step out of yours and look before stepping into the next. I did not eat either one. Over-cooked.",
      oathbroker: "A second writ follows the first by a beat. Those who escaped the first were served the second. Clear of both, in order. I sent my colleagues a reminder, after, in writing.",
      ironpalm: "Fire twice. The first, then the second. I did not run from the first into the second.",
      ranger: "Two cones, two of us, one after the other. Step out sideways from the first, then check the second. Don't hop from the pan into the other pan.",
      shadowblade: "The mark breathed twice, at two others, and I admired the timing. Do not leap out of the first into the second. I stood at its flank and watched. Impatient friends make fine examples.",
      pyromancer: "Two breaths, and not at me! Rude. The second one is a beat behind the first, so when I was marked I stepped sideways and then looked before I moved again. Fringe: intact.",
      thermist: "Breath, then breath. Step aside from the first, look, then step. I went from one into the other once and learned the word scald. Do not trust the gap between two fires.",
      driftblade: "Two fans of fire, one behind the other, and I cut across the first and through the gap and out the side of the second, round them both, and almost didn't see it.",
      marionettist: "The star breathed twice, at two different members of the cast, a beat apart. Leave the first spotlight and check the second before stepping. (The Doll was in the wings, as ever.)",
      cleric: "Two fires, one after the other, at two of my flock. One of them forgot to look before stepping, and I mended him with a smile and a very small sermon.",
      grovekeeper: "The first gust of fire, and a second right behind it. My lot stepped out of the first and into nothing, if they looked. Look both ways, always.",
      chronomancer: "The second breath follows the first. I left the first on its note, and stayed out of the second on its. The second was the hard one.",
      alembist: "Two breaths, a beat apart, at two targets: leave the first sideways, check the second, then move again. Label both flasks. First flask: now. Second: not yet.",
      threadweaver: "Two fans of fire, one behind the other. Step out sideways from the first, and check the second before pulling anyone through. Never mend over a smoking stitch.",
      tidecaller: "Two fans of fire at two of us, one right behind the other. We stepped sideways out of the first and looked before the next step, for the second lay waiting. The foam of the first wave hides the second.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two fans of fire, one hard on the heels of the other, each aimed at one of us. Sidestep the first, then look before you step again: the second was waiting. My lantern went clear of the first before I put it down.",
      kenseier: "Two fans of fire at two of us, the second a heartbeat after the first. We watched the first go and did not step into the second. Read the second stroke before leaving the first.",
    } },
    'Heart Burn': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Heart opened and he could not be hurt. Scorch marks bloomed at the three pits one after another, and the last one marked where the fire would not reach. The line stood round it, off the mark. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The Heart opened and nothing could touch him. Three scorches came up in turn; the last is where the great fire does not fall. We gathered round it, never on it. The first two taught me which.",
      thresher: "His heart opened and nothing of ours would bite. Scorches came up at the pits one after another, and the last one is the only ground the great fire spares. I stood close round it, off the scorch, and waited for it to fall.",
      bloodreaver: "His heart opened in his chest, and I could not touch him. Three marks, one after another, and the last is where the fire does not go. I stood by it, and not on it, and bled for nothing at all.",
      runeguard: "Three marks bloom in turn, and the third is where the great fire will not fall. Once the first two are known, the third is known; stand around it, but not on it. A proof by induction, written in embers.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast opened his heart, and we were not to touch. Three little dishes were set out in turn, and the last was the only seat the fire did not reach. We sat round it. Not on it. Rich.",
      oathbroker: "The Heart is a stay of proceedings: no claim may be made against him. The last of the three marks is the island, the only ground not forfeit. The adjudication is by observation.",
      ironpalm: "His heart opened. I could not strike. Three marks came in turn; the last is the quiet place. Stand beside it, not on it.",
      ranger: "Heart open, untouchable. Three scorches in order. The last one is where the fire stops. Stood around it, not on it. Easy to hit nothing. Hard to wait.",
      shadowblade: "The mark opened its heart, which was almost indecent. Three scorches, and the last is the only quiet ground. We stood round it, not on it. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "His heart opened and I could not burn it, which was an insult! Three scorches bloomed, and the last was the quiet place in the fire. I stood round it, not on it. Mostly unsinged, just.",
      thermist: "Heart open, nothing to hit. Three marks, in turn. The last is the cold place. Stand round it, not on it. I stood on it once and learned the word scorch. Not that middle. The other middle.",
      driftblade: "His heart lay open and I could not cut it, so I flew. Three scorches bloomed in turn, the last the quiet place, and I was there before the fire, circling the mark.",
      marionettist: "Act the last. The star opens his heart and all the footlights go out but three, and the last of them is the only light that stays lit. The cast must stand round it, not in it.",
      cleric: "His heart opened, and no blow of ours could touch him. Three marks, and the last was the quiet place in the fire. I called my flock round it, not on it. We were all there, and I counted.",
      grovekeeper: "A fire comes over everything but the one place. Three marks, in turn, and the last is the clear ground. My lot stand round it, not on it. Know where the last is.",
      chronomancer: "Three marks, three beats, and the last is where the fire does not fall. I knew from the second mark which it would be. I stood there before it bloomed. I did.",
      alembist: "A floor-wide fire, three marks in turn, one island. The last mark is the island; stand round it, not on it. Label the third flask. Third flask: safe. Others: not.",
      threadweaver: "The fire came over the whole floor but one hole in it. Three marks, and the last is the hole. I drew everyone to it, round the mark and not on it. Whip-stitched the edge, in my head.",
      tidecaller: "The Heart opened and he could not be hurt. Three scorches bloomed one after another, and the last marked the one island the great fire would spare. Its quiet ground is wider than the scorch, so we gathered round the mark, never on it, with the water brimming. Wait for the water.",
      lanternkeeper: "The Heart opened and he could not be hurt. Three scorches bloomed one after another, and the last marked the one island the great fire would spare. We gathered round the mark, never on it, and I kept the wicks full against the heat.",
      kenseier: "The Heart opened and he was beyond harm. Marks of scorch bloomed at the three pits in turn; the last named the place the fire would spare. We stood about it, never on it. Ash settles.",
    } },
    'Wingbeat': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "His wings beat as he flew, and everything near the line was shoved away from it. I stood far enough from the line that it only nudged me, and held my footing. A man can be moved. The trick is to be asked politely.",
      gloomsworn: "The wingbeat threw everything near his flight line outward. I stood well back from it and felt only a nudge. Nearer, you meet the wall; I keep a good distance from both.",
      thresher: "His wings stirred a gale down the dive road, and anyone near it was flung like ash from a pyre. I stood well back from the road, not just off it, and kept clear of the walls besides.",
      bloodreaver: "A shove from his wings, and everyone near the line goes flying! I like a good shove. Stay well back from the line, or be carried away from it, or into the wall.",
      runeguard: "The wings lift as he enters the line. Those near it are thrown outward, so stand well back from it, or be ready for the wall. A footnote for the wall, in red.",
      gorgemaw: "The wings carried a gust that threw the diners from the table's middle. I took my plate to the far end, well away from the line, and stayed seated. Blown, not eaten.",
      oathbroker: "The Wingbeat is a rider to the dive: all parties near the line are removed from it by force. The question is where they are removed to. Stand well back, or choose the landing.",
      ironpalm: "His wings spoke, and everything near the line was pushed away. I stood far from it, and was not pushed.",
      ranger: "Wingbeat. Everyone near the line is thrown clear of it. Stood off, well back, no push. Simple, if you like walls.",
      shadowblade: "His wings threw everyone near the line aside, which was rude of him. I was already far from it, naturally. Neither he nor the wall saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He flapped, and the gust threw my hair back and my friends across the floor! Stay well back from the line, or be thrown into the wall. Windswept, but flattering.",
      thermist: "Wingbeat: cold air, everyone near the line thrown out. Stay far from it, not just clear of it. I stood close once, and swung, and hit the wall. Wall: hard. Noted.",
      driftblade: "His wings threw a gust across the line and I rode it, out along the push and through the gap at the end, round and away, never landing, and not into the wall.",
      marionettist: "The star's wings fanned the stage and threw the cast to the wings. Stand well back from his entrance, or be carried off. (My Doll was blown into the orchestra. Unhurt.)",
      cleric: "His wings threw my flock away from the line, and one met the wall and cried out. Stand well back, not merely clear, I tell them now, and I mended what I could.",
      grovekeeper: "The wings stirred a gale along the line and everyone near it was blown away like leaves, dear. Stand well back, and the gale only ruffles. Keep from the edge, too.",
      chronomancer: "The gust comes just before the dive itself, and everyone near the line is thrown. I stood far back at that moment. Last time, which was next time, I stood closer, and learned.",
      alembist: "The Wingbeat, a gust along the line that throws everyone near it. Stand well back, or accept the wall. The wall is not a cure. I have the bruise to prove it.",
      threadweaver: "His wings pulled every thread near the line out sideways, and one stitch hit the wall. Stand well back from the line, and keep clear of the edge.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights his wings shoved everyone near the flight line outward, toward the wall. Well back is not the same as merely clear: we stood far off from the line and felt only a nudge. Big water pushes everything near it.",
      lanternkeeper: "The hard nights added a wingbeat that shoved anyone near his flight line out toward the wall. Merely clear was not enough; far was. We stood far, felt a nudge, and I kept my lanterns back there with us.",
      kenseier: "His wings beat as he flew, and everything near the line was shoved away from it. I stood far enough from it that it only nudged me. A push is not a wound. Let it carry you, then plant your feet again.",
    } },
    'Cinder Rain': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Embers fell across the crater, each in a little circle that burned afterwards. I held my ground, watched for the circles, and moved only when one landed near me. A man under rain does not need to run.",
      gloomsworn: "Embers fell everywhere in small circles, each leaving a burning patch. I walked clear of whichever came near me and no further. There was no hurry in it.",
      thresher: "Embers dropped in small circles all over the crater, each leaving a burning pool. I kept moving, watched where the next would rise, and went round. Plenty of fresh pits out there to fall into; I took none.",
      bloodreaver: "Sparks! All over the floor, and each leaves a pool. A scar for every one, if you like, and I do not, today. Keep moving, and watch the little circles.",
      runeguard: "Small circles bloom one after another across the whole crater. Each leaves a pool, so watch where the next one appears, and do not stand still. I read the pattern on the third; the first two were tuition.",
      gorgemaw: "A shower of sparks from the great oven. Little circles of heat, each leaving a puddle of sauce. I ate none, and moved along the table. Over-seasoned.",
      oathbroker: "The Rain is a series of small claims, each against a named tile, each leaving a lien on the ground. They come one after another. Clear each in turn.",
      ironpalm: "Embers fell, one at a time, all over. Each left a small fire. I stepped aside from each in turn.",
      ranger: "Cinder rain. Small circles, one after another, all over the floor. Each leaves a pool. Kept moving, kept clear.",
      shadowblade: "Sparks fell all over the crater, and each left a pool. A fine thing to watch, from the shadows. I kept moving, between the circles.",
      pyromancer: "It RAINED fire! Little circles blooming all over the floor, each one leaving a puddle of lava! I have never been so happy or so busy. Hem: gone. Spirit: soaring.",
      thermist: "Embers, hot, small, many. Step aside from each and off the pools. I ran hot with joy and stepped in one. Carefully, now.",
      driftblade: "The embers fell all over the crater and I wove between them, through the gaps, round the pools, never stopping, and the floor behind me burned in my footsteps.",
      marionettist: "A shower of sparks from the flies, each landing in a small bright circle, and each leaving a hot patch on the boards. The cast kept to the dark. (The Doll adored it.)",
      cleric: "Embers fell in little circles all over the floor, each leaving a pool. Nobody stood still, which for once I had no need to preach.",
      grovekeeper: "The embers came like seeds scattered by a careless hand, and where each fell, a pool of fire. Keep moving, step round them, and plant nothing there. My lot did.",
      chronomancer: "Each ember lands after the one before. I stepped aside from the first, and the next, and the next. I kept the rhythm, mostly.",
      alembist: "Many small circles, each leaving a pool: keep moving, step clear of each in turn. Do not shelve anything on the floor. This is a rule now.",
      threadweaver: "Embers fell like pins across the whole floor, each a small hole in the cloth. Step clear of each, and off the pools they leave.",
      tidecaller: "Embers fell all over the crater, each leaving a small pool that burned. I kept walking, stepped round each as it landed, kept off the pools afterwards, and fed the water in little drops. Rain is patient, and so are we.",
      lanternkeeper: "Embers fell all over the crater, each leaving a small pool that burned. I kept walking, stepped round each one as it landed, and kept my lanterns off the pools. Nobody stood still under that rain.",
      kenseier: "Each ember had its small circle drawn before it fell, and burned the ground after. I stood quiet and stepped aside only for the ones that came near. Rain asks no one to run, only to be elsewhere.",
    } },
    'Heart Flare': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The Heart flared. One of us was marked for a hit we had to share, so I closed the line on them; and the moment it landed we scattered, each to our own patch. Two orders in one breath. The line took both.",
      gloomsworn: "A ring over one of us to be shared, so we closed in on it. The instant it landed there were rings over everyone, and we went apart, each to a space of our own. First gather, then leave.",
      thresher: "First a ring over one of us to share, so we all stood in close, one cloak among many. The moment it fell, a ring over each head, so out to our own ground. Together, then apart, one step after the other.",
      bloodreaver: "A flare! First everyone piles onto the one who is marked, then we all fly apart before the second blow. A fine scar, and a good dance.",
      runeguard: "A ring over one of us, to be shared; and the instant it lands, marks over all. Together first, then apart. The order is the whole text, and I underlined it twice.",
      gorgemaw: "A course for the table, then dessert for each. First all sit down together at the marked seat and share it, then each rises and takes a plate of their own. Rich.",
      oathbroker: "The Flare is a two-part instrument: a shared debt, then individual claims. The order is everything. Stand together, then stand alone.",
      ironpalm: "Once to all of us in the ring. Then to each of us alone. We stood together first, then apart.",
      ranger: "Ring on one of us, share it. Then marks on everyone. Together, then apart.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flared, and I stood in the ring with the rest, which I hate, and then I went to my own corner as fast as I could, which I love.",
      pyromancer: "A shared flare, and then marks over everyone's head! Together first, then apart, as fast as possible. I forgot the second part once. Fringe: gone.",
      thermist: "Ring: shared, hot. Everyone in. Then marks: out, apart, each alone. I swung between the two and ran hot, then cold. Out. Apart. Then out some more.",
      driftblade: "The ring came and I hated standing in it with everyone, then the marks came and I swept out of the ring and across the floor and through the others' patches, to my own.",
      marionettist: "Curtain call in two parts: the whole troupe bows together, and the moment it ends, each steps downstage alone. (The Doll was always alone. It's a gift.)",
      cleric: "He flared. First we stood together in the ring, and the hit was shared; then marks fell on each of us, and we scattered. I mended the ones who forgot the second half.",
      grovekeeper: "First the weather gathers us in, and the flare breaks over the lot; then it breaks again over each alone. Together, then scatter. Know which half is next.",
      chronomancer: "The second part lands just after the first, and I moved on that note. The first is shared, the second is not. I stood in the ring a beat too long once.",
      alembist: "A shared dose, then a separate one for each: together first, then apart. Keep the flasks in two groups. Group one: shared. Group two: personal. No mixing! Ever.",
      threadweaver: "The first was shared, so I drew every thread in tight; the second was for each, so I cut them loose the instant the first landed. A neat piece of cutting.",
      tidecaller: "A ring over one of us to be shared, and we closed on it together. The moment it landed, marks fell over every head and we scattered, each to our own ground. In, then out, like the tide, and a good singer knows which half she is in.",
      lanternkeeper: "A ring over one of us, to be shared, and we closed in on it together. The moment it landed, marks fell over every head and we scattered, each to our own ground. In first, then out. I left a lantern at the ring and walked away from it.",
      kenseier: "The Heart flared. One of the others was marked for a blow that wanted sharing, so we closed on them, and once it landed we parted, each to a patch of our own. Together, then apart: two beats.",
    } },
  },
};
